WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
The Apollo 11 moon landing was one of&nbsp;
the boldest milestones in human history.&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.080
Yet it almost didn’t happen.
On July 20, 1969, three minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.600
before Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin stepped&nbsp;
foot on the lunar surface, something went wrong.&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.800
The computer set off alarms&nbsp;
warning of an emergency.&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.960
Error codes popped up on-screen.
NASA faced a tough decision:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.520
Should they continue or cancel the mission?
Had the agency decided to abort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.520
it would have been a crushing blow for&nbsp;
NASA, heart-breaking for the nation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.160
and would have created a window of opportunity&nbsp;
for the Russians to get to the moon first.&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.840
In the end, the astronauts landed safely.
Thanks to a young, brilliant software engineer.&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.160
Margaret Hamilton developed NASA’s&nbsp;
in-flight software for Apollo.&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.160
She spent her entire career focused&nbsp;
on anticipating problems and solving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.840
them which saved the moon landing.
Her penchant for detecting errors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.840
came from an unexpected place. After an&nbsp;
incident involving her young daughter.&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:05.920
Hamilton was born 33 years earlier, on August&nbsp;
17, 1936, in the small town of Paoli, Indiana.&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.800
She earned a degree in mathematics with a&nbsp;
minor in philosophy from Earlham College.&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.360
She taught math at high school for a year before&nbsp;
following her husband to Cambridge, Massachusetts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.960
where he attended Harvard Law School.
To support her husband’s studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.640
she took a job at MIT to write software&nbsp;
that could predict the weather.&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.920
Software engineering was not yet a field - people&nbsp;
didn’t go to school to learn how to program.&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.200
Hamilton taught herself.
She later wrote software to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.040
detect enemy aircraft for the military and then&nbsp;
planned to get a PhD in abstract mathematics.&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.400
But one day, her husband noticed a newspaper ad&nbsp;
that a lab at MIT was looking for programmers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.560
to help take people to the moon.
NASA had contracted that lab to build the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.160
onboard flight software for Apollo.
Hamilton jumped at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.680
opportunity to join the team.
She was the first programmer they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.240
hired. A woman in a heavily male world.
Hamilton began writing code for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.680
portable computer responsible&nbsp;
for controlling the spacecraft.&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.480
The Apollo Guidance Computer engineered&nbsp;
at MIT was a technological marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.000
given computers back then were the size of rooms!
One unit was aboard the command module Columbia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.200
that took astronauts to and from Earth, the&nbsp;
other was aboard Eagle, which landed on the moon.&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.280
At first, NASA executives assigned&nbsp;
Hamilton low-level tasks since she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.600
was one of the least experienced programmers.
Her first assignment was to write code that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.920
would kick in if the mission had to abort.
The higher-ups were so sure this wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.240
happen that she named her program “Forget It”&nbsp;
because she didn’t think any of the emergencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.360
she planned for would ever see the light of day.
Her software needed to detect unexpected errors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.240
quickly and recover from them in real-time.
However, it was very difficult to do because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.480
the software at the time could only&nbsp;
process jobs in a predetermined order.&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.760
So, she developed what’s called&nbsp;
“asynchronous programming”.&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.840
This allowed the computer to recognize&nbsp;
error messages and ignore low-priority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.800
tasks in favor of more important ones.
So even if there were an error, the high-priority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.040
tasks would continue to be performed.
This is a photo of her beside a stack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.560
printed code that’s nearly as tall as her.
She had insisted on adding more code as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.720
safeguard to prevent a crash from happening&nbsp;
after an incident with her young daughter.&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.200
She brought her four-year-old to work one&nbsp;
day, and Lauren played astronaut and started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.400
hitting the keys which crashed the system&nbsp;
during a simulation of a mission to the moon.&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.560
Her daughter had selected a program that was&nbsp;
meant to be used pre-launch which caused the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.800
computer to lose its navigation data.
Hamilton thought to herself:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.360
“...my God – this could inadvertently happen&nbsp;
in a real mission” as she told the Guardian.&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.720
And if that happened, the&nbsp;
crew could be lost in space.&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.160
So, she asked her bosses for&nbsp;
permission to add more fail safes.&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.120
At first, they refused. It was more&nbsp;
software to debug and work with.&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.040
Plus, they didn’t think an astronaut&nbsp;
would make that kind of mistake.&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.320
But that mistake happened&nbsp;
on the very next mission.&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.680
During Apollo 8, the first&nbsp;
manned mission orbiting the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.240
experienced astronaut Jim Lovell said: “Uh,&nbsp;
oh. I think I just did something wrong.”&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.240
He had accidentally pushed the wrong button which&nbsp;
started the pre-launch program and created havoc.&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:15.120
Hamilton and her coders spent nine hours&nbsp;
painstakingly troubleshooting the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.960
and fortunately, managed to send new&nbsp;
navigation data to the spacecraft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.520
guiding the astronauts back home safely.
After that nightmare, Hamilton’s higher-ups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.080
let her put more safeguards into the software.
By this time, she had worked her way up from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.760
junior engineer to lead software&nbsp;
design for the Apollo program.&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.240
Everyone realized Hamilton’s ability&nbsp;
to detect errors and recover from them.&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.440
The team got the greatest scare&nbsp;
during the Apollo 11 mission.&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.520
Just as Armstrong and Aldrin were&nbsp;
about to land on the lunar surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.240
the computer started flashing error codes.
Hamilton was standing in the monitoring room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.240
at MIT when she saw what was happening.
They later discovered that a radar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.040
switch was in the wrong position and took up&nbsp;
processing power, overloading the computer.&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:03.360
The moon mission was at risk of being aborted.
However, NASA trusted Hamilton’s system.&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.720
They believed it was well-designed&nbsp;
and could come to the rescue.&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.000
And it did.
Her code prioritized the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:18.720
tasks and allowed the mission to stay on track.
The software worked perfectly and both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.000
astronauts landed safely on the moon.
It was a moment that hit Hamilton hard.&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.680
She recalled saying: “My God. Look what&nbsp;
happened. We did it. It worked. It was exciting.”&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.680
Hamilton popularized the&nbsp;
term “software engineering”.&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.840
At the time, software was not taken as&nbsp;
seriously as other disciplines in engineering.&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.400
She helped launch an industry that&nbsp;
is now changing human history.&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.840
Margaret Heafield Hamilton&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.920
In 2017, President Barack Obama awarded Hamilton&nbsp;
the United States’ highest civilian award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:55.760
the Presidential Medal of Freedom.
President Obama said:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.240
“Our astronauts didn’t have much time, but&nbsp;
thankfully they had Margaret Hamilton.”&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.600
It’s thanks to curious minds that we’re&nbsp;
able to venture out into the solar system.&nbsp;

